<h1>Password Salting Explained Without the Confusion</h1>
<p>A salt is a random value added to the password hashing process. It is not a secret password, not an encryption key, and not a magic fix for weak algorithms. Its job is to make each stored password hash unique, even when two users choose the same password. That small design choice makes many attacks harder.</p>
<p>OWASP explains salting in its <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a>. A unique salt forces attackers to attack hashes one at a time instead of calculating one hash and comparing it across every user. It also prevents simple visual detection of users who share the same password.</p>
<p>Imagine two users both choose the password spring2027. Without a salt, the same algorithm produces the same stored hash for both users. Anyone looking at the database can see that the two accounts share a password, even without knowing what the password is. With unique salts, the stored hashes differ because each password is processed with a different random value.</p>
<p>A salt does not need to be hidden like a secret key. It is commonly stored with the password hash. That may feel strange at first, but it is normal. The salt works because it changes the input to each hash, not because attackers cannot see it. Modern password hashing formats usually store the algorithm, parameters, salt, and hash together.</p>
<p>Salting is not enough by itself. A salted MD5 hash is still a fast hash. An attacker can read the salt, test candidate passwords with that salt, and compare the result. That is slower than attacking unsalted hashes in bulk, but it is still far weaker than bcrypt, Argon2id, scrypt, or PBKDF2 with appropriate cost settings.</p>
<p>This is why salt and work factor should be discussed together. The salt protects against shared hashes and precomputed tables. The work factor slows each guess. Argon2id adds memory cost as well. Bcrypt adds a cost parameter. PBKDF2 repeats a pseudorandom function many times. Each method is trying to make offline guessing less attractive.</p>
<p>Developers should usually let their password library generate salts automatically. Manually generating salts can lead to mistakes such as short salts, reused salts, predictable salts, or salts stored in a separate table that later gets lost. If your framework has a standard password hashing function, use it first and customize only when there is a documented need.</p>
<p>The word unique is important. A global salt reused for every user is not the same as a per-password salt. A predictable salt such as the username is also weaker than a random one. Modern libraries solve this cleanly, which is why application code should not usually build salt handling by hand.</p>
<p>When reviewing an old database, look for whether the salt is per user or global. A single site-wide salt sometimes appears in legacy code as a secret constant. That is closer to a pepper than a proper salt, and it does not provide the same protection as unique random salts for every password.</p>
<p>The clean takeaway is this: every password should have a unique random salt, but the salt must be paired with a proper password hashing algorithm. If your system stores salted MD5 values, it is better than unsalted MD5, but it still needs a migration plan. For the bigger picture, read <a href="/argon2id-password-hashing.html">Argon2id Password Hashing</a> and <a href="/bcrypt-password-hashing.html">Bcrypt Password Hashing</a>.</p>
